*Scoping* | V-$CHIPVR ($CHIPCN)

Every variable belongs to one of three scopes: the file's globals, a function body, or a block. Where you declared it decides which one it is, and that decides how long it lives and who can read it.

*Scope Levels*

''Global Scope''   - Declared at the top level of a file, outside every block.

''Function Scope'' - Declared in a function body.

''Block Scope''    - Declared inside an ''if'', ''while'', or ''for'' body.

*Global Scope*

A variable or function declared at the top level of a file is available for the rest of that file, and to every file pulled in with ''load(filename)''. Globals are shared by name, which is how a program built from several files fits together:

'''
var greeting = "hello";

func Greet()
{
    # this reads the global
    return greeting;
}
'''

*Function Scope*

A variable declared in a function body is available only while that call runs, and only inside that function:

'''
func DeclareLocal()
{
    var local = 20;
}

DeclareLocal();

# local is gone
var x = local;
'''

Every call gets its own locals, so a function that calls itself does not tread on the values of the call that started it.

A function written inside another function can use the outer function's variables. Both sides work on the same variable, so each sees what the other writes:

'''
func MakeCounter(start)
{
    var n = start;

    return func()
    {
        n = n + 1;

        return n;
    };
}

# result is 2
var result = MakeCounter(1)();

'''

Each call to ''MakeCounter(start)'' gets its own ''n'', so two counters made this way do not affect each other.

*Block Scope*

A ''var'' declared inside an ''if'', ''while'', or ''for'' body can be used for the rest of that body, but not after it. This holds at the top level of a file too: a ''var'' inside a block is never a global.

'''
if true
{
    var y = 10;

    # same block, thus fine
    var doubled = y * 2;
}

# y is gone here
var z = y;
'''

The ''for'' variable belongs to its loop and is not available afterwards:

'''
for i = 1 to 3
{
    # i is 1, 2, 3
}

# i is gone here
var n = i;
'''

A block may reuse a name from further out. It is a separate variable, and the one outside is unchanged once the block ends:

'''
var v = "outer";

for v = 1 to 2
{
    # v counts 1, 2 in here
}

# v is "outer" again
'''

*Writing to a Name*

Only ''var'' declares. Writing to a name without it looks outwards for a variable that already exists and writes to that one, so a block or a function can update state around it:

'''
var count = 0;

for i = 1 to 3
{
    # writes to the count above
    count = count + 1;
}

# count is 3
'''

If a name doesn't exist anywhere, there is nothing to write to and the program fails hard:

'''
# Not declared, thus runtime error
undeclared = 1;
'''

